[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="SkyCyclePreset" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://godot/island/scripts/SCRIPT_sky_cycle_preset.gd" id="1_preset"]

; NORMALCY, AND THE ONTOLOGICAL NIGHTMARE. The whole of SCENE_test_zone_W2's sky as
; one resource, so it can be dropped on another scene instead of copied between
; .tscn files. `docs/DOC_w2_blood_night.md` is the write-up.
;
; TWO STATES AND A FLIP BETWEEN THEM, not a day and a night. The "day" here is
; ordinary overcast daylight and is supposed to be unremarkable; the "night" is the
; world going wrong — the dome above blacks out, the cumulus ring and the cloud sea
; stay lit and turn deep red, and everything takes its light from those two. The
; controller crosses between them in about three seconds off a bool. Nothing is
; meant to sit in between.
;
; THE TRANSITION BAND IS THE FLIP'S OWN MIDDLE. It is not a dusk anybody stands in:
; at `nightmare_seconds` 3.0 across a span of 0.40 on the clock, the 0.12-wide
; sunset band below is about 0.9 s of ember while the sky is crossing over. That is
; why its colours are hot and slightly unreal rather than a plausible sunset — it
; is only ever seen in motion, and it is the half-breath of warning before the red.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DAY IS 100% AND IT IS THE ANCHOR, so it is the block to be most careful with.
; A white sun at full energy is what SCENE_test_zone_W2 rendered before any of this
; existed — because its TimeOfDay bus was never wired and the island was lit by
; project.godot's fallbacks — and it is what "normalcy" has to mean here. The whole
; effect is a CONTRAST, and it only works if the thing being contrasted against is
; the plain daylight everyone already knows this scene by.
;
; `day_sun_color` white and `day_sun_energy` 1.0 are therefore not a tuning, they
; are a restoration, and they are why a fully-lit top face is 1.31 and not the 0.41
; the scene's own comment derived. That derivation was correct about what the
; authored values MEANT and wrong about what was on screen; the picture won. The
; ambient and bounce COLOURS are still the ones it produced.
;
; THE AMBIENT'S LEVEL WAS NOT, and it lagged the sun for a long time. At 0.30 it
; was the level that derivation set against a quarter-power sun, left in place when
; the sun went back to full — so by day the sky gave a wall 0.1 of light against the
; sun's 1.0, and every face the fixed sun never reaches (the pod's far side, the
; wreck's, the ruin's door) drew black, which is not what "ordinary overcast
; daylight" looks like. `day_ambient_energy` is 0.60 now, doubled when the shadows
; were asked to come up, alongside the env ball's ambient floor going from 0.35 to
; 0.6 (`SCRIPT_bake_env_ball.gd`). Measured in the island at the pod: its shadow
; side's median palette entry went from 17 of 255 to 51, while flat sunlit grass
; went up by a fifth (a mean of 85 to 103), because an ambient lights everything
; the sky can see and grass sees the most of it. The sun is untouched.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE NIGHT, multiplied out the way the scene comment does:
;
;   tod_sky_color    = night_ambient_color * 0.32 = (0.320, 0.024, 0.018)
;   tod_ground_color = night_bounce_color  * 0.32 = (0.294, 0.027, 0.021)
;   tod_sun_color    = night_sun_color     * 0.21 = (0.210, 0.018, 0.012)
;
; A fully-lit top face lands near 0.53 RED against 0.04 green and 0.03 blue, which
; is 13:1 — the saturation, and the reason this reads as blood rather than as an
; orange filter. Against a 1.31 day it is also about 40% of the light, and that
; drop is wanted now: it is not a dark night, it is a much redder and somewhat
; dimmer world, and the flip between the two has to be felt.
;
; WHY THE NIGHT'S BOUNCE IS BRIGHTER THAN ITS SKY, inverting the day: at noon the
; dome overhead is the bright thing and the cloud sea below is darker, and
; `SHADERINC_island_light.gdshaderinc` lerps between them by the world normal's Y. At night
; the dome is BLACK and the lit things are the ring and the sea, so the light comes
; from the horizon and from below and a cliff face picks up more of it than a
; fairway does.
;
; SATURATION AND LEVEL ARE ONE DIAL, not two. Saturation of the light is
; (max - min) / max and red is pinned at 1.0, so the only way up is taking green and
; blue DOWN — which lowers luminance as a side effect, because green carries 71% of
; it. Pushing the red further from here does not need a matching darkening; it is
; one.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IS NOT IN HERE, and it is the reason this travels at all: `sky_haze_base`,
; `sky_glow_base` and the rest stay on the controller node. W2 hazes to the exact
; neutral 0.30 and SCENE_test_zone_W hazes to 0.089; both are right, each is a
; property of that scene's own sky and fog, and neither is a property of the
; weather. The tints below are MULTIPLIERS on whichever a scene holds, so this same
; night lands correctly on either without knowing which it is in. To put it on W:
; add a SkyCycleController, point `sky_haze_base` at W's 0.089, set `sky_material`
; to MAT_sky_void.tres, turn on `drive_void_sky` and `use_nightmare_switch`, and
; assign this. Nothing here has to change.

; CANDY LAND's day, at the user's request: SKYCYCLE_w2_blood_night with its
; day made candy — a warm white sun a little stronger, a sky-blue ambient, a
; pinkish bounce off the ground, and a sky-blue dome at every phase (the
; island is held at midday, manual_time_of_day 0.45, so only the day is seen).

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_preset")

day_start = 0.10
day_end = 0.60
night_start = 0.72
night_end = 0.98

day_sun_color = Color(1, 0.97, 0.9, 1)
day_sun_energy = 1.0
day_ambient_color = Color(0.62, 0.72, 0.88, 1)
day_bounce_color = Color(0.5, 0.42, 0.5, 1)
day_ambient_energy = 0.65
day_sky_contribution = 1.0
day_sky_brightness = 1.0
day_shadow_opacity = 1.0
day_sun_rotation_degrees = Vector3(-60, -30, 0)

trans_sun_color = Color(1, 0.44, 0.16, 1)
trans_sun_energy = 0.55
trans_ambient_color = Color(0.82, 0.36, 0.28, 1)
trans_bounce_color = Color(0.6, 0.24, 0.16, 1)
trans_ambient_energy = 0.28
trans_sky_contribution = 0.6
trans_sky_brightness = 1.0
trans_shadow_opacity = 0.7
trans_sun_rotation_degrees = Vector3(-5, 30, 0)

night_sun_color = Color(1, 0.085, 0.055, 1)
night_sun_energy = 0.21
night_ambient_color = Color(1, 0.075, 0.055, 1)
night_bounce_color = Color(0.92, 0.085, 0.065, 1)
night_ambient_energy = 0.32
night_sky_contribution = 0.2
night_sky_brightness = 1.0
night_shadow_opacity = 0.0
night_sun_rotation_degrees = Vector3(60, 150, 0)

day_haze_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
trans_haze_tint = Color(0.88, 0.48, 0.38, 1)
night_haze_tint = Color(0.82, 0.135, 0.1, 1)
day_art_tint = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
trans_art_tint = Color(1.05, 0.6, 0.46, 1)
night_art_tint = Color(1.4, 0.17, 0.13, 1)

day_sky_top = Color(0.3, 0.6, 0.98, 1)
trans_sky_top = Color(0.3, 0.6, 0.98, 1)
night_sky_top = Color(0.3, 0.6, 0.98, 1)
